commit cbda9eaf091cce9365842e7c86172b80cccabcea
Author: Alexander Williams <awill@opentitan.org>
Date:   Fri Feb 9 17:42:33 2024 -0800

    [spi_device] Fix up TPM command handling

    Move the TPM Write and Read FIFOs to the SRAM. Change the Write FIFO's
    interface to be more like the SPI flash upload type, where the hardware
    side is a FIFO, but the software side is a RAM with an explicit
    busy/free bit. As a side effect, the Write FIFO now has a wider
    interface, and buffer reads can proceed more rapidly.

    Adjust the synchronization mechanisms with software to eliminate
    ambiguity in the relationship between data buffers and commands.

    For TPM write commands, wait until the data is completely transferred
    before pushing the command into the command FIFO. In addition, do not
    pass the wait state if the command FIFO is not empty and if the write
    FIFO has not been released back to hardware. Aborted write commands will
    simply be dropped, and software does not get confusing information. The
    process works like this:

    0. Write command enters the SPI hardware.
    1. HW waits for the command FIFO to be empty and for the payload buffer
       to be released.
    2. HW proceeds past wait states until just before the last posedge for
       the last bit specified by the command's byte count (xfer_size_met).
    3. HW writes the command to the command FIFO on that last posedge. The
       SRAM is also written here for the payload.
    4. SW reads the command from the command FIFO and notes the length of
       the data. A write command being present signifies the full data is in
       the write FIFO.
    5. SW reads the data from the write FIFO, then writes the TPM_STATUS CSR
       to release the buffer back to HW.

    For TPM read commands, wait until the command FIFO is empty before
    pushing a new read command into it. In addition, hold the Read FIFO in
    reset on the sys_clk side until a new read command is popped from the
    command FIFO. "New" means a command that was pushed since CSB was most
    recently asserted. After this handshake, the Read FIFO's reset is lifted
    and becomes open for writing. The process works like this:

    0. RdFIFO is held in reset. It must be empty and can't be written yet.
       Read command enters the SPI hardware.
    1. HW waits for SW to empty the command FIFO.
    2. HW writes the read command to the command FIFO. This should happen
       basically instantly for software (relative to the command FIFO
       emptying).
    4. Software reads the command FIFO to lift the RdFIFO reset. This gate
       prevents SW from writing stale data.
    5. HW waits for the RdFIFO to have enough data.
    6. HW completes the command.
    7. HW puts the RdFIFO back in reset once CSB de-asserts.

    In addition to those mechanisms, add a new interrupt to signal when data
    was dropped from the RdFIFO. This includes both writes occurring while
    the RdFIFO is held in reset and a read command not transferring its
    proscribed number of bytes. It does not include data that software wrote
    into the FIFO beyond what the command called for.

    Signed-off-by: Alexander Williams <awill@opentitan.org>

